    File d = new File("file/blu/bla/testdir");
    // Create directory and all of its non-existent parent directories.
    d.mkdirs();
    mkdirs {

        getParent
        boolean a = parent.mkdirs() || parent.exists() // recursive call to "mkdirs"
        if ((parent != null) && a && mkdir()) // IF not null AND parent created AND this dir created THEN all went fine
    }

http://stackoverflow.com/questions/2395465/create-a-file-in-linux-using-c
You need the three-argument form of open() when you specify O_CREAT. When you omit the third argument, open() uses whatever value happens to be on the stack where the third argument was expected; this is seldom a coherent set of permissions.
The third argument is the permissions on the file - which will be modified by the umask() value.
int fd2 = open("/tmp/test.svg", O_RDWR | O_CREAT, S_IRUSR | S_IRGRP | S_IROTH);
Note that you can create a file without write permissions (to anyone else, or any other process) while still being able to write to it from the current process. There is seldom a need to use execute bits on files created from a program - unless you are writing a compiler (and '.svg' files are not normally executables!).
The S_xxxx flags come from <sys/stat.h> and <fcntl.h> — you can use either header to get the information.

https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22949500/create-file-inside-a-directory-c
use sprintf() or similar to create the pathFilename string:
char pathFile[MAX_PATHNAME_LEN];
sprintf(pathFile, "%s\\my_log.txt", directory );
then
int filedescriptor = open(pathFile, O_RDWR | O_APPEND | O_CREAT);
Note: If you are using linux, change \\ to / and MAX_PATHNAME_LEN to 260 (or whatever linux likes to use for that value.)
EDIT if you need to check that a directory exists before creating a file there, you can do something like this:
if (stat("/dir1/my_dir", &st) == -1) {
    mkdir("/dir1/my_dir", 0700);
}
Read more here: stat, mkdir
